 "stories": [
  {
   "region_code": "luzon",
   "title": "The Legend of Daragang Magayon",
   "body": "Long ago in Bicol lived Daragang Magayon, a maiden whose name means 'beautiful lady'. She fell in love with Panganoron, a brave chieftain's son, but a jealous suitor named Pagtuga waged war to take her. In the battle both lovers were lost, and they were buried together. From their grave rose a mountain of perfect beauty: Mayon Volcano. Its clouds are said to be Panganoron embracing his beloved, and its eruptions the anger of Pagtuga."
  },
  {
   "region_code": "visayas",
   "title": "The Legend of the Chocolate Hills",
   "body": "One telling from Bohol says that two giants once quarreled across the land. For days they hurled rocks and sand at each other until they grew tired, made peace, and left the island together. They never cleaned up after their battle, and the scattered mounds became the Chocolate Hills. The legend reminds Boholanos that even a great quarrel can end in friendship."
  },
  {
   "region_code": "mindanao",
   "title": "The Darangen",
   "body": "The Darangen is the epic of the Maranao people of Lake Lanao. Sung over several days, it tells of the hero Bantugan and the kingdom of Bembaran, of courage, love, and the customs that bind a community. In 2005 UNESCO recognized the Darangen as a Masterpiece of the Oral and Intangible Heritage of Humanity."
  },
  {
   "region_code": "pearl",
   "title": "The Pearl of the Orient",
   "body": "With the Luzon, Visayas and Mindanao Cores restored, their light joins together over the seas. Thirty voices become one song, many languages, one people. You have unlocked the Pearl of the Orient, a symbol of Filipino unity, identity and heritage. Keep speaking, keep listening, and keep these languages alive."
  }
